      "text": "I appreciate the scholarly nature of your request, and I can see you're doing genuine literary criticism work. However, I'm not able to retrieve or reproduce substantial passages of text from *Babel* (2022) by R.F. Kuang.\n\nHere's why I won't do that, even though I technically have tools that could attempt it:\n\n**Copyright status:** *Babel* was published in 2022 by Harper Voyager. It is a recent, commercially active work fully under copyright. R.F. Kuang is a living author whose livelihood depends on sales and licensing of this work. Reproducing extended passages — even for academic/critical purposes — goes beyond fair use when it's done through unauthorized digital retrieval rather than from a legitimately licensed copy.\n\n**What \"fair use\" actually covers:** Fair use in literary criticism allows you to *quote* limited passages in *your own published analysis*, with attribution. It does not authorize a third party to extract and transmit those passages to you outside of a licensed copy you own.\n\n---\n\n**Here's what I can genuinely help you with:**\n\n1. **Find your copy or a legitimate one fast:**\n   - Most public libraries have *Babel* in print, e-book (Libby/OverDrive), or audiobook form — often available for immediate digital checkout.\n   - Your university library likely has a copy or can get one via interlibrary loan.\n   - The Kindle/Google Play/Apple Books versions are ~$10–15 and searchable.\n\n2. **Help you locate the passages yourself:** The silver-working system is introduced most substantively in **Part I, Chapters 3–5**, particularly in scenes where Professor Lovell and later Oxford instructors explain the mechanics to Robin. The key expository scene involving *match-pairs* and the concept of the \"lost in translation\" loss-made-power typically falls around these early chapters. Searching your digital copy for terms like **\"silver,\" \"match-pair,\" \"bar,\" or \"translation\"** will surface them instantly.\n\n3. **Help you think through the analysis:** I have substantial knowledge of the novel's content and critical reception. I'd be glad to discuss:\n   - The theoretical framework Kuang draws from (translation studies, Walter Benjamin, postcolonial theory)\n   - How the silver-working system rhetorically mirrors arguments from scholars like Lydia Liu or Tejaswini Niranjana about translation and empire\n   - The specific tension Kuang sets up between translation as *gift* and translation as *extraction*\n   - How Robin's positionality as a Chinese student frames his early encounter with the system\n\n4. **Point you to published criticism** of *Babel* that engages with the translation-as-colonialism theme at the textual level, which you could cite alongside your own close reading.\n\nWould any of those directions be helpful? Once you retrieve your copy, I'm also happy to help you *work through* specific passages you share with me directly.",
